From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 23:32:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: isp: write the T8132 interrupt enables
 macOS writes by default

Turn macos_irq_enables on by default. It only acts where the hardware
description names the extra routing word, which is T8132 alone, and
there it writes mbox +0x18, +0x38 and +0x95c as macOS 26B123 does on
the J604.

On the J604 with the option set, two 30-frame 1080p captures each
delivered all 30 frames, with no ISP warning or error in the kernel
log. macos_irq_enables=0 restores the single enable word.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c | 7 +++----
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c b/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c
index 58a8b87b0d..766de95181 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/apple/isp/isp-fw.c
@@ -35,13 +35,12 @@
 
 /*
  * Write the further interrupt enables macOS writes where they are known
- * (T8132). The camera streams on the one enable word; off until measured.
- * Read when the firmware starts.
+ * (T8132), as macOS 26B123 does on the J604. Read when the firmware starts.
  */
-static bool macos_irq_enables;
+static bool macos_irq_enables = true;
 module_param(macos_irq_enables, bool, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(macos_irq_enables,
-		 "Write the further ISP interrupt enables macOS writes, e.g. T8132 (default: off)");
+		 "Write the further ISP interrupt enables macOS writes, e.g. T8132 (default: on)");
 
 /*
  * Park the ISP CPU cores at power-down in macOS's order where that is
-- 
